A||N||6||TRL46202||Need Help!||||1||
Z||000000||TRL46202||03-21-2000||08:31 AM||||How do you repair/glue the console covers on the dash,around the shifter etc. They seem to be turning up around the edges where the hard palstic inlays go and I would like to reglue, but don't know where to start???<P>Thanks!!<P>Tom||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phoenix||03-21-2000||12:43 PM||||I just completed doing this to the radio console, shifter console and the center armrest console on my 88. The glue to use is a polyurathane type, when this glue cures it "foams" to fill voids. The brand I used was Gorilla Glue. These types of glues are available at any good woodworking store.  Use a short piece of stiff wood and several clamps to distribute the pressure over the surface of the piece and work with one edge at a time. It will take several days to complete all of the edges but the results look as good as new.||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||03-21-2000||12:49 PM||||Another thing that works well is clear silicone caulk...it fills voids well and pretty much sticks to anything.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BAM-BAM||03-22-2000||08:54 PM||||  Hey Phoenix, how hard of a hold does the Gorilla Glue have? In your opinion, will it last a good while? I just had a post of this nature and I want to do this right.<P>  Thanx, BAM OUT! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000004||Monkeyman||03-22-2000||09:39 PM||||None of my covers need to be glued, but I HAVE to get some Gorilla Glue (for obvious reasons)!!  Where do I get some??  Anyone have a spare tube??||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||03-22-2000||09:51 PM||||When you clamp the sticks to the sides to hold them flat while the glue is drying wrap the stick with wax paper to keep any oozing glue from making a mess with the stick getting stuck to the part.<P>Try saying that really fast three times.||||reg||3
Z||000006||Phoenix||03-23-2000||08:12 AM||||BAM-BAM - Polyurathane glues are typically used by professional wood workers for strong joints and it is also used in the sign making industry for gluing-up large outdoor sign blanks.  I have used it for a variety of applications and it holds well and doesn't soften in the heat.||||reg||1
